## A B S T R A C T
Nanobody(Nb) is too small to carry more signal reporters, which often leads to low sensitivity in immunoassay. Herein, we proposed a novel immunoprobe integration of Nb and biomimetic mineralized metal-organic frameworks(MOF), in which plenty of succinylated horseradish peroxidase(sHRP) were encapsulated within a single MOF and the Nb was assembled on the biomimetic mineralized MOF. It overcomes the dilemma that the Nb is difficult to carry more signal reporters. Meanwhile, the mineralized MOF can protect the sHRP from denaturation and facilitate the transport of substrates to the active sites of sHRP. Electrosensing of aflatoxin $\mathbf{B}_{1}(\mathrm{AFB}_{1})$ was realized with a competitive format in which the target $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ and immobilized artificial antigen were competing for binding with the immunoprobe. Additionally, the detection signal was enlarged by the catalysis of this immunoprobe to 4-chloro-1-naphthol for producing precipitations, which blocked the channels of the immunoprobe and the redox probes of $\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}^{3-/4-}$ was difficult to reach the electrode surface through the channels. Hence, the as-prepared immunosensor exhibited good voltammetry responses towards the determined $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ in a linear range of $50.0\mathrm{fg/mL}\cdot20.0\mathrm{ng/mL}$ with a detection limit of $20.0~\mathrm{fg/mL}$ . The specificity, stability, and reproducibility of this immunosensor were satisfactory. This work may provide an alternative idea for the application of Nb in immunoassay, and the idea may also be applicable to other bio-recognition elements for immunoassay.
## 1. Introduction
Nanobody(Nb) is cloned from the variable domain of Camelidae species’ heavy-chain antibody and is famous for its small size, good stability, and water solubility(Guo et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2020; Tang et al., 2017). However, it is difficult to label signal reporters on the Nb owing to the small size and single-domain nature, resulting in low signal density in immunoassay. Several strategies have been reported for improving the detection sensitivity of Nb in immunoassay(Li et al., 2017, 2020; F. Wang et al., 2020). For example, polymeric horseradish peroxidase(poly-HRP) was adopted as a reporter to increase the signal output(Li et al., 2017; Liu et al., 2020), while it still has some concerns containing expensive, poor stability, large steric hindrance to the Nb and unavoidable loss of the activity of Nb or poly-HRP during the coupling process. Therefore, it is very promising to develop a novel labeling strategy that can increase the number of signal reporters conjugated to Nb.
Porous materials have attracted much attention in the construction of immunoprobes due to their large specific surface areas for loading a high amount of signal reporters(Sharma et al., 2021; Tang and Ma, 2017). In a variety of porous materials, MOF has gained a good repu­ tation for its highly ordered porous and controllable structures(Feng et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2019). Recently, researchers have found that biomaterials such as proteins, DNA, viruses, and even bacteria can induce the growth of MOF in an aqueous solution(Doonan et al., 2017; Liang et al., 2015). This amazing discovery, defined as “biomimetic mineralization”, can protect the encapsulated biomolecules in MOF from degradation or denaturation in harsh environments via robust coatings (Liang et al., 2015; Maddigan et al., 2018). Nowadays, bio­ mimetic mineralized MOF for enzymes immobilization(usually expressed as enzyme@MOF) has attracted wide attention because of the mineralized MOF coating not only protects the enzymes from unfavor­ able conditions but also facilitates selective transport of substrates to the active sites of the encapsulated enzymes via the ordered pore network (Liang et al., 2021). Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8(ZIF-8) is the most widely studied MOF for this aim because it can be synthesized under biologically compatible conditions without damaging enzyme activity (Liang et al., 2015; Maddigan et al., 2018). However, the ZIF-8 is a hydrophobic MOF, which will engender conformational changes that denature and lead to loss of activity for the encapsulated enzymes(Liang et al., 2019). Zeolitic imidazolate framework-90(ZIF-90) and metal azolate framework-7(MAF-7), which have received less attention in this field, are featured with good hydrophilicity and show good biocom­ patibility with the encapsulated or absorbed proteins(Liang et al., 2019; L. Wang et al., 2020; Wu et al., 2020). Based on this perspective, Wang et al. reported a probe of aptamer-capped HRP $@$ MAF-7 for developing a novel colorimetric aptasensor, in which the embedded HRP catalyzed the substrates to produce a detectable signal by naked eyes, and the aptamers modified on the shell of the probe through π-π stacking and electrostatic interactions were responsible for target recognition(L. Wang et al., 2020). Besides, Wu et al. modified the HRP@ZIF-90 with specific peptides via Schiff’s base reaction between the aldehyde group and the N-terminal of peptides, then the peptide-functionalized $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}\ –90$ was utilized as a probe for sensing an important biomarker(Wu et al., 2020).
Motived by the background knowledge above, a new immunoprobe was proposed here based on the assembly of Nb on the biomimetic mineralized MOF(Scheme 1A). This is a core/shell/shell-like structure with the MOF coating sandwiched. The “core” consists of numerous sHRP and the outermost “shell” consists of Nb. This immunoprobe was put forward to develop an electrochemical immunosensor for detection of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ , a typical mycotoxin presented in foods. The immunoprobe carrying plenty of sHRP provided a stronger detection signal for the immunosensor through the catalysis of 4-chloro-1-naphthol(CN) to produce precipitations, which then hindered the redox probes close to the surface of the electrode. Benefiting from this design, the constructed electrochemical immunosensor showed good performance in sensitivity, stability, and reproducibility. Moreover, flour and rice samples were evaluated by this immunosensor with satisfactory results, indicating this immunoprobe’s value in practical application.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Modification of HRP with succinic anhydride
The preparation of succinylated HRP (sHRP) was as follows(Maddi­ gan et al., 2018). First, $200~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of HRP was dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of bi­ carbonate buffer(0.13 M, pH 8.5). Succinic anhydride $\mathrm{(100mg)}$ was then added to the above solution in gradual increments, and the pH of the solution shall not be lower than 8.0 in the whole process. Then, the solution was gently stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at room temperature, following by dialysis against $5.0\mathrm{~L~}$ of phosphate buffer saline(PBS) and deionized water, respectively.
## 2.2. Synthesis of sHRP@MAF-7 and MAF-7
Typically for the synthesis of sHRP $@$ MAF-7, 40 mL of zinc nitrate(50 mM) was added to a $40~\mathrm{mL}$ solution of 3-methyl-1, 2, 4-triazole(Hmtz) 0 $\mathrm{250~mM}$ containing $60~\mathrm{mg}$ sHRP, following by addition of ammonium hydroxide( $10\%$ , W/V) for triggering the reaction. After stirring for $24\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature, the products were collected by centrifugation and washed with excess deionized water to ensure the removal of sHRP loosely attached to the surface of MAF-7. Finally, the as-obtained $s\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MAF}{\cdot}7$ were dried under vacuum and weighed before they were re-dissolved in deionized water. The synthesis of bare MAF-7 was similar to the above except for no addition of sHRP.
## 2.3. Assembly of Nb on sHRP@MAF-7
Anti- ${\bf\cal A F B}_{1}$ Nb(named as G8) was prepared as previously described (Zhang et al., 2022). The as-prepared Nb was added to the solution of sHRP@MAF-7 with a final concentration of $50\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . Then, the mixture was gently stirred at room temperature for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ and the obtained products were marked as Nb@sHRP@MAF-7, which was the immu­ noprobe proposed in this study. After removing excess Nb by centrifu­ gation, $0.5\%$ ovalbumin(OVA) was used for blocking the immunoprobe.
## 2.4. Procedure of the electrochemical immunosensor for $A F B_{1}$ analysis
Scheme 1B displays the stepwise fabrication and principle of the immunosensor. Briefly, AuNPs was electroplated onto a polished glassy carbon electrode(GCE) through cyclic voltammetry(CV) in 0.25 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S{\cal O}_{4}$ containing $1\mathrm{\mM\HAuCl_{4}}$ (potential of $-0.2\:\mathrm{V}_{-0.6}\:\mathrm{V}$ , scan rate of $10\ \mathrm{mV/s}$ , one circle). After rinsing with deionized water and drying under a nitrogen stream, ${5\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of $\mathrm{AFB_{1}{\cdot}B S A}(40\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ was dropwised on the surface of the GCE and incubated at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for overnight. This modified GCE was then covered by $1\%$ OVA for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature to block the remaining sites and marked as OVA/AFB1-BSA/AuNPs/GCE. For detection of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ , an equivalent mixture of a specified concentration of $\mathsf{N b}@\mathsf{s H R P}@\mathsf{M A F}.7$ and desirable concentrations of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ was incubated with the $\mathrm{OVA}/\mathrm{AFB_{1}}$ -BSA/AuNPs/GCE for $30\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. After sufficient washing with Tris-HCl buffer $\mathrm{.10~mM}$ , pH 7.5), the 4- chloro-1-naphthol(CN) solution was added to produce precipitations for voltammetric assay.
Other parts containing Materials&Reagents, Apparatus, Fluorescein-labeled sHRP and Nb, Synthesis of enzymes@ZIF-8 and enzymes ${\bf\boldsymbol{\omega}}\mathbf{ZIF-90}$ , Activity assay and kinetic parameters determi­ nation of sHRP@MAF-7, and Sample preparation were presented in the Supporting Information section.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Necessity for modification of HRP with succinic anhydride
Doonan’s group has systematically studied the effects of the elec­ trostatic properties of proteins on the synthesis of biomimetic mineral­ ization of MOF(Maddigan et al., 2018). It was demonstrated that modification of surface lysine residues with anhydrides facilitated the biomimetic mineralization by introducing more negative charges on the surface(indicating a decreased value of isoelectric point), whereas modification of surface carboxylate moieties with ethylenediamine increased the number of positive charges on protein(indicating an increased value of isoelectric point) and hindered the process of bio­ mimetic mineralization. Thus, they proposed that the electrostatic properties of proteins were a precise predictor of whether a protein will seed ZIF-8 growth in an aqueous solution. Since the electrostatic prop­ erties of proteins are a key factor, the surface charge of HRP should be considered for the effective encapsulation of HRP within MOF.
Until now, at least 42 isozymes of HRP have been identified from plants, and they were classified into three categories, acidic-, neutral-, and basic-isozymes according to their isoelectric point(pI) (Hoyle, 1977; Kamal and Behere, 2008). Among these isozymes(Table S1), neutral HRP is the main component of commercial HRP preparation from horseradish plants because of its high yield, stability, and activity(Kamal and Behere, 2008; Veitch, 2004). Herein, the pI of the HRP employed in this study was specified as 7.2 by the provider. We modified the natural HRP with succinic anhydride to acquire the succinylated HRP(sHRP) for the synthesis of enzyme $@$ MOF. As can be seen from Fig. S1A, no obvious precipitations were induced when the natural HRP participated in the biomimetic mineralization of ZIF-8 under a classical stoichiometric ratio of metal ions and organic linker(Liang et al., 2019), whereas obvious precipitations were induced when the sHRP in participation. During the synthesis of ZIF-90 and MAF-7 under the classical ratio, the production of precipitations did not depend on the participation of proteins(Liang et al., 2019). However, we found that when the HRP and sHRP were involved respectively in the biomimetic mineralization of ZIF-90 or MAF-7, the results were significantly different from each other. As shown in Figure S1B and Fig. 1A, the synthetic products of sHRP $@$ ZIF-90 and sHRP $@$ MAF-7 exhibit a transparent supernatant, and the supernatants of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}\ –90$ and HRP@MAF-7 are light brown. These results indicated that the enzyme@ZIF-90 or enzyme@MAF-7 synthesized from sHRP exhibited a higher loading efficiency than that from natural HRP. We carried out the detection of supernatants of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MAF}{\cdot}7$ and sHRP $@$ MAF-7 using UV spectroscopy to confirm the loading efficiency. As it can be seen in Fig. 1B, the presence of the Soret absorption band at $405\mathrm{nm}$ is evidence that much iron-heme remains in the supernatant of HRP@MAF-7, and the absence of the Soret band for the supernatant of sHRP $@$ MAF-7 means that majority of sHRP were encapsulated in the MAF-7(L. Wang et al., 2020). We measured the zeta potential of HRP and sHRP in the presence or absence of 250 mM Hmtz precursor solution respectively to investigate their charge. The zeta potential data presented in Fig. 1C show that the sHRP coexisting or not with Hmtz in the solution both possess a lower value of potential than that of natural HRP. This can be attributed to that upon the modification with the succinic anhydride, some $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ and –OH(mainly in the glycan coat surrounding HRP) on the surface of HRP were converted to the carboxyl groups, which is more conducive to enriching metal ions and facilitating the encapsulation of enzyme by MOF(Maddigan et al., 2018).
## 3.2. Characterization of the immunoprobe
The morphology features of sHRP@MAF-7 were observed by scan­ ning electron microscope(SEM). As shown in Fig. 2A, the bare MAF-7 displays as a sphere on the micron scale(diameter around $2\mathrm{-}5\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ with a rhombic surface, which seems like the Echinopsis tubiflora (Vel´asquez-Herna´ndez et al., 2020). The morphology of $\mathsf{s H R P}@\mathbf{MAF}–7$ shown in Fig. 2B and C maintains the spherical structure, while the surface becomes smooth and the diameter increases. Fourier transform infrared(FTIR) spectroscopy and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) were performed to ascertain the incorporation of sHRP within the MAF-7. It can be seen from Fig. 2E, the vibrational mode at $425~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ found in MAF-7 and sHRP $@$ MAF-7, is assigned to the $z\mathrm{n-N}$ stretching vibration(Vel´asquez-Herna´ndez et al., 2020), which indicates the net­ works are formed with Hmtz and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ nodes through coordination. The bending vibration of $1480\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $700{-}660\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ found in MAF-7 and sHRP@MAF-7, are assigned to the characteristic modes of the azolate ligands(Liang et al., 2019; Vela´squez-Herna´ndez et al., 2020). In com­ parison with MAF-7, a new vibration band at $1664\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appeared in the sHRP $@$ MAF-7, which was ascribed to the amide I band of proteins(Liang et al., 2019; L. Wang et al., 2020), suggesting the incorporation of sHRP. The TGA results of MAF-7 and HRP $@$ MAF-7 also clearly verify the encapsulation of sHRP in MAF-7(Fig. S2), and the amount of sHRP encapsulated within MAF-7 was calculated to be approx. $20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ sHRP for sHRP $@$ MAF-7(Vel´asquez- Herna´ndez et al., 2020; L. Wang et al.,
2020). Furthermore, bare MAF-7 and sHRP $@$ MAF-7 were examined by X-ray diffraction(XRD), respectively. As shown in Fig. 2F, the XRD patterns show that both the bare MAF-7 and sHRP@MAF-7 are phase pure and possess the sod topology (Liang et al., 2019).
Confocal laser scanning microscopy(CLSM) was performed to investigate the spatial distribution of sHRP and Nb related to the MAF-7. At first, fluorescein isothiocyanate(FITC) labeled sHRP(F-sHRP) was used for the synthesis of F-sHRP@MAF-7. CLSM images of the F$s\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MAF}{\cdot}7$ are shown in Fig. 3B, the results reveal that the F-sHRP are localized in the center and around the inner surface region of MAF-7 coating. Next, rhodamine B isothiocyanate(RbITC) labeled Nb(Rb–Nb) was reacted with the F-sHRP@MAF-7 and the product Rb–Nb@FsHRP@MAF-7 was collected by centrifugation, following by sufficient washing to remove the loosely adsorbed Rb–Nb. CLSM images of the Rb–Nb@F-sHRP@MAF-7 shown in Fig. 3C show that the Nb is uniformly attached to the surface of the F-sHRP@MAF-7. The results indicated that the synthesis of a core/shell/shell-like immunoprobe was successful.
## 3.3. Catalytic performance of sHRP@MAF-7
We investigated the enzymatic characteristics of the sHRP $@$ MAF-7 by using 3, $3^{\prime}$ , 5, ${\bf5^{\prime}}.$ - tetramethylbenzidine(TMB) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as sub­ strates. The specific activities(SA) of sHRP and sHRP $@$ MAF-7 were calculated as $516.2\pm13.4~\mathrm{U/mg}$ and $137.5\pm1.1~\mathrm{U/mg}$ respectively (Fig. S3). The SA of sHRP $@$ MAF-7 is lower than that of sHRP, which can be due to the decreased purity of sHRP by introducing the MAF-7 coatings. We then measured the kinetic parameters of the sHRP@MAF-7. Results of steady-state kinetic assays shown in Fig. S4 reveal that when the amount of sHRP $@$ MAF-7 is kept constant and the substrate concentration is increased, the initial reaction velocity $(V_{0})$ increase until it reaches a maximum point(defined as $V_{\mathrm{max}})$ and then the reaction velocity will not continue to increase by increasing the con­ centration of substrates. The steady-state kinetic characteristics of sHRP are similar to the sHRP $@$ MAF-7 with typical Michaelis-Menten curves. Based on the different $V_{0}$ with variable substrate concentrations at a fixed concentration of $s\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MAF}{-}7$ , a Lineweaver-Burk plot can be obtained with an ideal linear relationship(Fig. S5), from which the ki­ netic parameters of $K_{\mathrm{cat}}$ and $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ can be obtained(Table S2). Overall, the apparent $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value of the sHRP $@$ MAF-7 with TMB or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as the sub­ strate was slightly lower than the sHRP, suggesting that the sHRP $@$ MAF7 has a slightly higher affinity for TMB or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ compared with sHRP, which is similar to other reports(Birhanli et al., 2022). The $K_{\mathrm{cat}}$ value, meaning the maximum number of substrate molecules turned over per catalyst molecule per unit time, gives a direct measure of the catalytic activity under a special condition. We estimate that the mass percentage of sHRP in the sHRP $@$ MAF-7 biocomposite is about $20\%$ according to the TGA results(Vel´asquez-Herna´ndez et al., 2020; L. Wang et al., 2020). Based on this, we calculated the $K_{\mathrm{cat}}$ values of sHRP $@$ MAF-7 for the TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to be $660.2~s^{-1}$ and $699.8~s^{-1}$ respectively, which are higher than the $K_{\mathrm{cat}}$ values of sHRP(Table S2). There are two possible reasons for these results: 1) the hydrophilic MAF-7 coating has little effect on the conformation of sHRP(Liang et al., 2019), thus maintaining a high af­ finity between the sHRP@MAF-7 and substrates, 2) the sHRP $@$ MAF-7 with high surface area, porosity and nanoconfinement, can accommo­ date the accessibility and retention of substrate molecules within the networks, which make the substrate molecules closer to the active sites of sHRP, causing the so-called “proximity effect” for high-efficiency catalysis(Zhang et al., 2020).
## 3.4. Principle of the immunoprobe for detecting $A F B_{1}$
The immunoprobe of $\mathrm{Nb}@\mathrm{sHRP}@\mathrm{MAF}.7$ was applied to construct an electrochemical immunosensor as illustrated in Scheme 1B. AuNPs electroplated on the GCE was used as a platform for attachment of the artificial antigen $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA), then the determination was realized with a competitive format in which the target $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA are competing for binding with the immunoprobe. After the washing step, the bound immunoprobe was put forward to catalysis of CN. The CN will enter into the immunoprobe through the porous channels and proximity to the sHRP, causing much precipitations to block the immunoprobe (Akter et al., 2012; Chen et al., 2019). Then, the exogenous redox probes of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}^{3-/4-}$ is not easy to reach the electrode surface through the immunoprobe, thus the current response to the redox probe decrease. Oppositely, scarcely any immunoprobe bound on the electrode when the concentration of target $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ is high, let alone any precipitations. At this moment, the current response to the redox probes increased. We have confirmed this through experiments and the results are shown in Fig. 4A, when $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ was detected at a concentration of $0.5\mathrm{ng/mL}$ with CN as the substrate, the peak current $(I)$ increased by $81.63\%$ compared with that at $0~\mathrm{{ng/mL}}$ . Without CN, the $I$ detected at the concentration of 0.5 $\mathrm{ng/mL}$ was only $56.12\%$ higher than that at $0\mathrm{ng/mL}$ . Furthermore, we also compared the performance of HRP-conjugated Nb with the pro­ posed immunoprobe when using CN as substrate. The results show that the I measured at the concentration of $0.5\mathrm{ng/mL}$ is only $48.19\%$ higher than that at $0\mathrm{ng/mL}$ of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ (Fig. S7). Besides, the I detected at $0\mathrm{ng/mL}$ of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ with the conjugate is obviously higher than that with the immunoprobe, which could be ascribed to that the limited amount of HRP loaded by the conjugate can hardly induce enough precipitations. All these results indicated that the proposed immunoprobe can work well.
## 3.5. Characterization of the electrochemical immunosensor
CV was conducted to monitor each reaction step on the GCE. As shown in Fig. S8A, a pair of well defined redox peaks of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}^{3-/4-}$ are observed at bare GCE(curve a). The redox current significantly increased after the electrodeposition of AuNPs on the electrode (curve b) due to the effective acceleration of electron transfer by AuNPs. When covered with $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA and OVA sequentially, the current response decreased in gradient because of the high resistance of protein layers which retarded the electron transfer. After reaction with the immunoprobe of $\mathrm{Nb}@\mathrm{sHRP}@\mathrm{MAF}.7$ , a remarkable decrease in peak current and increase in peak-to-peak separation $(\varDelta E\mathrm{p})$ can be found. When CN is catalyzed by the immunoprobe to form precipitations, the electron transfer at the electrode interface is seriously hindered and the reversible electro­ chemical redox reactions of ${\dot{\mathrm{Fe}}}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}^{3-/4-}$ become poor(Akter et al., 2012). Therefore, the peak current becomes very small and the $\varDelta E\mathrm{p}$ becomes larger, accompanied by serious deformation of the CV curve. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy(EIS) was also used to charac­ terize the interface behavior of various electrodes and the results are consistent with the CV.
## 3.6. Optimization of the immunosensor
The amount of Nb assembled on sHRP@MAF-7 was optimized for it is very important to the sensitivity of the proposed immunosensor. As illustrated in Fig. 4B, when the amount of Nb assembled on sHRP $@$ MAF7 is less, the binding ability of Nb@sHRP@MAF-7 to $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA is poor, resulting in less immunoprobes bound to the GCE and a higher peak current. On the contrary, if the amount of Nb on the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MAF}{\cdot}7$ is abundant, more $\mathrm{Nb}@{\mathsf{s H R P}}@\mathrm{MAF}.7$ would be captured by $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA due to its well-binding ability. However, it should be noted that when the Nb is excessive, a large amount of targeted $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ will be consumed to bind the excess Nb on the immunoprobe, resulting in the loss of competitiveness of the target $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ with $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA to the Nb and thus decreased sensitivity. Therefore, the concentration of Nb for assembly on $\mathsf{s H R P}@\mathbf{MAF}–7$ was chosen as $50~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ because of the higher $\varDelta I$ (which means higher sensitivity). Concentrations of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA and $\mathrm{Nb}@{\mathsf{s H R P}}@\mathrm{MAF}\cdot7$ , as well as their reaction time were optimized. Different concentrations of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA were immobilized on the surface of the AuNPs modified GCE and reacted with a specified concentration of the immunoprobe. When the concentration of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA is $40\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , the reduction peak current tends to be the lowest, which means that the amount of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA is saturated on the electrode(Fig. S9A). At the specified concentration of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA, different concentrations of the immunoprobe were added for reaction with the $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA. When the concentration of the immunoprobe is $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ , the peak current reaches the lowest, indicating that the reaction tends to be saturated (Fig. S9B). The reaction time of this immunoprobe with $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA was also optimized. As presented in Fig. S9C, the reaction between $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA and $\mathrm{Nb}@{\mathsf{s H R P}}@\mathrm{MAF}\cdot7$ tends to equilibrium at $30~\mathrm{min}$ , which can be attributed to the good biocompatibility of MAF-7 with Nb(Liang et al., 2019).
## 3.7. Analytical performance of the immunosensor
The performance of this immunosensor towards the determination of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ was evaluated by differential pulse voltammetry(DPV). Fig. 4C shows that the current responses of the immunosensor increased with the increasing concentration of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ . As the concentration of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ increased, more immunoprobes of $\mathrm{Nb}@s\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MAF}.7$ were eliminated by the competitive effects between the $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ -BSA and target $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ . Once the amount of Nb@sHRP@MAF-7 bound on the electrode is reduced, the precipitations generated by its catalytic activation of CN are also less, so the current becomes larger. Contrarily, the current will decrease obvi­ ously when there are more precipitations generated from the catalytic on CN with a larger amount of Nb@sHRP@MAF-7 due to the low con­ centration of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ . The calibration plot shown in Fig. 4D exhibits a good linear relationship between the peak currents $\left(I\mathfrak{p}\right)$ and the logarithm of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ concentrations in the range of $50.0~\mathrm{fg/mL}$ to $20.0~\mathrm{{ng/mL}}$ with a detection limit of $20.0~\mathrm{fg/mL}$ based on $s/\Nu=3$ . Quantitative $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ determination was carried out using the linear equation of $I=8.9422\mathrm{Ig}$ $[C_{\mathrm{AFB1}}]+18.6975(R^{2}~=~0.9961)$ . In comparison with other electro­ chemical competitive-type immunosensors for $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ (presented in Table 1), this immunosensor performed well.
## 3.8. Specificity, reproducibility, and stability of the immunosensor
Some potential interferences in real samples, including $\mathbf{AFB}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{A F G}_{2}$ , DON, OTA, ZEN, and patulin were selected to investigate the specificity of this immunosensor. As we have previously reported, the Nb(named G8, Fig. S6) employed here has no obvious cross-reaction with other mycotoxins as mentioned above(Zhang et al., 2022). Expectedly, the immunosensor proposed here exhibits a good specificity towards $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ (Fig. S10A). Six of the as-prepared electrodes were used to detect $\mathbf{50.0\pg/mL}$ of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ to evaluate the reproducibility of this immuno­ sensor. The relative standard deviation(RSD) of the current values is $0.81\%$ (Fig. S10B), indicating a good reproducibility. Furthermore, the stability of the immunosensor was also studied. After the immunosensor were stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 14 days, the current responses fell within $94.3\%$ (RSD of $2.7\%$ , ${\mathfrak{n}}=3{\mathrm{:}}$ ) of the initial value for detection of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ , suggesting a good stability.
## 3.9. Real sample analysis
Negative food samples spiked with different concentrations of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ were prepared(Li et al., 2013) and then detected using the proposed immunosensor to investigate the reliability in practical applications. The results are presented in Table S3. The immunosensor proposed here exhibits satisfactory recoveries ranging from $86.7\%$ to $96.5\%$ (RSD of $1.9\%6{-}6.1\%\big)$ ) for flour and $101.2\%-109.1\%$ (RSD of $2.7\%6{-}6.4\%$ for rice. These results indicated that the proposed immunosensor provided a potential application in the practical determination of $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ .
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, a new immunoprobe was proposed by integrating Nb and biomimetic mineralized MOF. Morphological studies indicated that the immunoprobe was a core/shell/shell-like structure with the MOF sandwiched. The “core” consisted of numerous sHRP, and the outermost “shell” consisted of Nb. The sHRP in the core of the immunoprobe enlarged the signal output through the catalysis of CN into precipitations and then improved the sensitivity of the immunosensor. In addition, the MOF coating showed good biocompatibility with the encapsulated sHRP and the assembled Nb. Benefiting from this design, this immunoprobe performed well in the electrochemical immunosensor for $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ . This work breaks through the dilemma of Nb’s unsatisfactory sensitivity in immunoassay because it can hardly carry more signal reporters due to the small size and single-domain nature, and meanwhile provides an alternative idea for application of other types of bio-recognition ele­ ments, such as aptamer, peptide, and antigen in bioanalysis.
